{"type":"message","id":"3ca90046","parentId":"d86bb9f1","timestamp":"2026-02-18T13:59:24.245Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_387222ac80e2460f844071b1","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"@echo off\nREM Install OpenClaw Gateway as a Windows Service using NSSM\n\nREM Check if NSSM is available\nwhere nssm >nul 2>nul\nif errorlevel 1 (\n    echo ERROR: NSSM (Non-Sucking Service Manager) is not installed or not in PATH.\n    echo.\n    echo Please download NSSM from: https://nssm.cc/download\n    echo Extract nssm.exe to a folder in your PATH (e.g., C:\\Windows\\System32) or specify full path below.\n    echo.\n    pause\n    exit /b 1\n)\n\nREM Install the service\nnssm install OpenClawGateway \"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\.openclaw-state\\workspace\\scripts\\gateway-wrapper.bat\"\n\nREM Configure service recovery: restart on failure\nnssm set OpenClawGateway AppRestartDelay 5000\nnssm set OpenClawGateway AppExit Default Restart\n\nREM Optional: Set service description\nnssm set OpenClawGateway Description \"OpenClaw Gateway - keeps the assistant running 24/7\"\n\necho.\necho Service installed successfully!\necho.\necho Start it with: net start OpenClawGateway\necho Or use: sc start OpenClawGateway\necho.\npause\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1771423164243}}
{"type":"message","id":"1911769a","parentId":"3ca90046","timestamp":"2026-02-18T13:59:32.531Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good! Now I have the contents. Let me analyze them for loops or self-restarting patterns:\n\n1. gateway-wrapper.bat:\n```\n@echo off\nREM Gateway wrapper script for Windows Service\nREM This script runs the OpenClaw gateway in the foreground\n\ncd /d \"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\"\nopenclaw gateway start\n```\nThis script simply changes to the directory and runs `openclaw gateway start`. No loops, no goto, no while, no start /b, no ping -n, no timeout /t. It's a simple wrapper. However, note that it's being run as a Windows Service via NSSM. The service might cause restarts if it exits, but that's not in the script itself. The script itself doesn't have any self-restart logic.\n\n2. install-service.bat:\n```\n@echo off\nREM Install OpenClaw Gateway as a Windows Service using NSSM\n...\nnssm install OpenClawGateway \"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\.openclaw-state\\workspace\\scripts\\gateway-wrapper.bat\"\n...\n```\nThis script installs the service. It doesn't have loops either. It checks for nssm, then installs the service and configures recovery to restart on failure. The restart behavior is set via nssm, not via the script.\n\nSo neither script contains explicit loops or self-restarting patterns in the code itself. However, the install-service.bat configures the service to restart on failure (AppRestartDelay 5000, AppExit Default Restart). That means if the gateway-wrapper.bat exits with an error, the service will restart it after 5 seconds. That could cause a loop if the gateway keeps crashing.\n\nBut the question is: \"Identify the specific file and the command/process that is re-triggering the gateway.\" The command that triggers the gateway is in gateway-wrapper.bat: `openclaw gateway start`. That command is executed by the Windows Service (OpenClawGateway) which is managed by NSSM. The service itself is what re-triggers the gateway if it fails. But the service is not a script file; it's a Windows Service registration. However, the service is installed to run the gateway-wrapper.bat script. So the file responsible for launching the gateway is gateway-wrapper.bat.\n\nBut the re-triggering is done by the service manager (NSSM), not by a loop in the script. The script itself just runs once. If the gateway process exits, NSSM restarts the service, which re-runs the wrapper script. So the \"command/process that is re-triggering the gateway\" could be considered the Windows Service control manager (sc or nssm). But the specific file that contains the command is gateway-wrapper.bat.\n\nThe user also asked: \"Check if any of these scripts are registered as scheduled tasks or services (the full output from previous commands may contain them).\" I've already identified that install-service.bat registers a service using nssm. The service name is OpenClawGateway, and it's configured to restart on failure. That is the registration. I should also check if there are any other scheduled tasks, but I only have these two scripts.\n\nNow, I also need to check the skills directory - I need to confirm if there are any script files there. The third command gave no output, but that could mean the directory doesn't exist or there are no .bat/.cmd/.ps1 files. I should check if skills exists and list non-script files too? The prompt specifically asks for .bat, .cmd, .ps1 files. If the directory exists but has no such files, that's fine. But I need to verify existence.\n\nLet me run a separate check for the skills directory:","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me verify the skills directory existence and check for any script files (maybe it exists but has no .bat/.cmd/.ps1 files):"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a1afcd2b9c6340d08ea0bf41","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"powershell -Command \"Test-Path 'D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\.openclaw-state\\workspace\\skills'\"","pty":true}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_fe3fe9a7a42c461aa1829687","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"powershell -Command \"if (Test-Path 'D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\.openclaw-state\\workspace\\skills') { Write-Output 'EXISTS' } else { Write-Output 'NOT_FOUND' }\"","pty":true}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_c259e909c04047e8b281a398","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"powershell -Command \"Get-ChildItem -Path 'D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\.openclaw-state\\workspace\\skills' -Recurse | Select-Object FullName\"","pty":true}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free","usage":{"input":22001,"output":1931,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":23932,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1771423164245}}
